course: dictionary
lesson: 14234
title: Nausea and feeling sick
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Nausea is a noun. It is the feeling that you are going to be sick, as if your tummy is turning over. You can say you have nausea, or that you feel nausea. Nausea is not the same as being sick: it is the warning feeling that comes before.
explain: «After the bumpy bus ride, Sam felt a wave of nausea and had to sit down.» A wave of nausea means the sick feeling came on suddenly. The bumpy bus is a clue that this is travel sickness, which is one common cause of nausea.
explain: Nausea also has a formal meaning: a strong feeling of disgust. «The smell of the rotten fish filled her with nausea.» Nobody has to be sick here. The word tells you she felt very disgusted by the smell.
explain: In the UK people often say «I feel sick». In the US people often say «I feel nauseous». Both mean the same thing. Use the clues in the sentence to find out which meaning the writer chose.
fact: Nausea is a noun, and it is uncountable. We say some nausea, not a nausea.
fact: «Nausea» means the feeling that you are going to be sick.
fact: Nausea is a symptom: a sign that your body is unwell, for example with food poisoning.
fact: Car sickness and sea sickness can both cause nausea on a journey.
fact: Phrases to know: a wave of nausea, feel nausea, travel nausea.
exercise: «After the rough boat trip, Ravi felt nausea and had to sit down.» What is nausea? | A sunny day at sea | A type of fish | *A feeling that you are going to be sick | A loud noise from the boat
exercise: «Her tummy turned over and she felt a wave of nausea.» What does a wave of nausea mean? | A big wave splashing on the beach | A small tiredness after a run | A happy, bubbly feeling | *A sudden feeling of being about to be sick
exercise: Which of these is a symptom of illness? | *Nausea | A loud laugh | A clean pair of shoes | A new bicycle
test: «The smell of the rotten fish filled her with nausea.» Here nausea means | *Strong disgust | Feeling very happy | Hunger | Tiredness after a run
test: Someone in the US says «I feel nauseous». What would someone in the UK say instead? | I feel fine | I feel hot | I feel sleepy | *I feel sick
exam: Which sentence uses nausea correctly? | I nausea every day. | I have a nausea. | *I have some nausea after the bumpy ride. | The nausea is a dog.
